<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<openrocket version="1.11" creator="OpenRocket 26.xx-SNAPSHOT-5f164fd0e">
  <rocket>
    <name>3D printable nose cone and fins</name>
    <id>ef6182bf-cd8a-46f9-a5ee-0a30ac588f9c</id>
    <axialoffset method="absolute">0.0</axialoffset>
    <position type="absolute">0.0</position>
    <comment>This rocket has two 3D-printable parts: a fin can, and a nose cone. Export the fin can and its children as one .OBJ file. Export the nose cone without its child (a screw eye) as an .OBJ file.

You&#39;ll add a BT-50 body tube, parachute or streamer, screw eye and shock cord. The motor is retained by friction fit: a couple wraps of masking tape around the motor should secure it.

Print the fin can out of filament with a higher melting point than PLA. Some good options are PETG and ABS. The motor mount should withstand the heat of paper-cased motors, but not aluminum-cased reloads.

The fin can can prints well on its forward end, with 20% infill, concentric support and a 30mm 3-layer brim to ensure stability. The nose cone is an easy print on its base, with 20% infill and a 12mm 3-layer brim for stability.</comment>
    <designer>David Cain, adapted from &quot;A simple model rocket&quot; by Sampo Niskanen</designer>
    <designtype>original</designtype>
    <revision>September 26, 2023, Version 1.1 - surface roughness, friction fit
September 15, 2023, Version 1.0</revision>
